<Base
  title="Wood Badge Ticket | Will Mau"
  description="Will Mau's Wood Badge ticket as Committee Chair of Scouting America Troop 3054: five goals that leave the troop running without depending on any one adult, with the evidence for each."
>
  {/* Shared by link with ticket reviewers; kept out of search results. */}
  <meta slot="head" name="robots" content="noindex, follow" />
  <main id="main" class="wb-page">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <SiteHeader />
      <div class="wrap page-hero__inner">
        {/* Wood Badge's traditional symbols (beads, woggle, tartan); no official emblem. */}
        <div class="wb-banner" data-reveal>
          <div class="wb-banner__body">
            <p class="wb-banner__kicker">Wood Badge &middot; Twin Rivers Council 2026</p>
            <h1 class="wb-banner__title">Working my ticket</h1>
            <p class="wb-banner__note">
              I chair the committee of Troop 3054, Mohawk District, Twin Rivers
              Council. My Wood Badge ticket is five goals, each a written process
              the committee owns, so the troop runs well without depending on any
              one adult, including me. This page is the evidence, updated as the
              work moves.
            </p>
            <dl class="wb-meta">
              {meta.map(([k, v]) => (
                <div>
                  <dt>{k}</dt>
                  <dd>{v}</dd>
                </div>
              ))}
            </dl>
          </div>

          <Image
            class="wb-banner__beads"
            src={woggleBeads}
            alt="Wood Badge beads on a leather thong, with the leather woggle"
            widths={[280, 560]}
            sizes="(max-width: 720px) 120px, 272px"
            loading="eager"
          />

          <ol class="wb-trail" aria-label="The road to the beads">
            {trail.map((t) => (
              <li class={`wb-trail__step wb-trail__step--${t.state}`}>
                <span class="wb-trail__dot" aria-hidden="true">
                  {t.state === 'next' && (
                    <>
                      <span class="wb-trail__bead"></span>
                      <span class="wb-trail__bead"></span>
                    </>
                  )}
                </span>
                <span class="wb-trail__label">{t.label}</span>
                <span class="wb-trail__when">{t.when}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* What Wood Badge is: stands on its own for readers outside Scouting. */}
    <section id="about-wood-badge" class="wb-about" aria-labelledby="about-wb-title">
      <div class="wrap wb-about__grid">
        <div class="wb-about__text">
          <p class="wb-label">New to Wood Badge? &middot; Since 1919</p>
          <h2 id="about-wb-title" class="wb-h2">What Wood Badge is</h2>
          <p>
            Wood Badge is Scouting's advanced leadership course for adult
            volunteers. It began with the first course at Gilwell Park,
            England, in September 1919, and Scouting associations around the
            world have run it ever since.
          </p>
          <p>
            The course is only the start. Each participant writes a ticket: a
            short set of personal goals, reviewed and approved by course staff,
            that puts what the course teaches to work back home. There are 18
            months to finish it. My course ran over two weekends at Camp
            Wakpominee, and this page is my ticket.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finishing the ticket earns the Wood Badge itself: two wooden beads
            on a leather thong, worn with the Gilwell neckerchief and its
            MacLaren tartan patch, and a leather woggle.
          </p>
          <p class="wb-about__skip">
            <a href="#status">Already a Wood Badger? Skip to where each goal stands <span aria-hidden="true">&#8594;</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="wb-about__photos">
            <Image class="wb-about__print wb-about__print--main" src={gilwell1919} alt="The first Wood Badge course, seated and standing around a campfire, Gilwell Park, 1919" widths={[480, 700]} sizes="(max-width: 860px) 90vw, 480px" loading="lazy" />
            <Image class="wb-about__print wb-about__print--inset" src={regalia} alt="Wood Badge beads and woggle on a tan Gilwell neckerchief with its tartan patch" widths={[320, 641]} sizes="(max-width: 860px) 50vw, 280px" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="wb-about__caption"><strong>Gilwell Park, September 1919:</strong> the first Wood Badge course. <strong>Inset:</strong> what the ticket earns, the beads, Gilwell neckerchief and woggle.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="wb">
      <div class="wrap wb__layout">
        <nav class="wb-toc" aria-label="On this page">
          <p class="wb-toc__label">On this page</p>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#about-wood-badge">What Wood Badge is</a></li>
            <li><a href="#vision">Vision</a></li>
            <li><a href="#status">Where things stand</a></li>
            {goals.map((g) => (
              <li><a href={`#${g.id}`}><span class="wb-toc__n">{g.n}</span>{g.short}</a></li>
            ))}
            <li><a href="#systems">Systems built</a></li>
            <li><a href="#shaped">How the ticket was shaped</a></li>
            <li><a href="#next-year">Who runs it next year</a></li>
            <li><a href="#on-request">Available on request</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div class="wb__main">
          {/* Vision */}
          <section id="vision" class="wb-sec">
            <p class="wb-label">My personal vision of success</p>
            <div class="wb-prose wb-vision">
              <p>
                Troop 3054 should be able to run well without depending on any
                one adult, including me. My vision is a troop where the Scouts
                plan and lead the program, where the committee's job is money,
                logistics, permits, drivers and paperwork rather than deciding
                what the Scouts will do, and where every key responsibility has
                more than one person who knows how to carry it.
              </p>
              <p>
                That means leaving behind systems, not projects: a recruitment
                cycle the pack and the troop run together every year, a Patrol
                Leaders' Council that plans the program the way Scouting America
                intends, a written record of who holds what and who backs them
                up, a budget process the committee owns, and one weekend a year
                where the older Scouts teach the younger ones what this is
                supposed to feel like. Each of those lives somewhere findable,
                with a name on it and a date it gets reviewed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <p class="wb-pull">
              The measure of success is not what I run while I am here. It is
              what keeps running after I stop.
            </p>
          </section>

          {/* Status */}
          <section id="status" class="wb-sec">
            <div class="wb-head">
              <p class="wb-label">Status as of {asOf}</p>
              <h2 class="wb-h2">Where each goal stands</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="wb-lede">
